      "title": "Fractional CFO Economics: Positioning, Pricing & Engagement Design",
      "module": "The CFO Mandate & Strategic Finance Leadership",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>The Business Model of Fractional CFO Work</h2><p>Running a fractional CFO practice is itself a financial services business - and the irony is that many fractional CFOs are excellent at advising clients on business model design while running their own practices on instinct and hope. The same principles that make a client company financially healthy apply directly: unit economics, pricing power, customer concentration risk, and scalability. CFOs who understand and apply these principles to their own practice build sustainable, premium-margin businesses. Those who don't spend their careers trading time for money at rates that don't reflect the value they create.</p><p>The fundamental unit economics of fractional CFO work are straightforward: your revenue is (number of clients) × (monthly retainer per client). Your cost is (your time) + (any team or admin overhead). Your profit is the spread. But the leverage in this model comes entirely from pricing - whether you charge $3,000 per month or $18,000 per month for what is essentially the same engagement type. The difference between those two numbers is not primarily experience. It's positioning, scarcity signals, and the ability to articulate value in terms clients can connect to their P&L.</p><h2>The Positioning Spectrum</h2><p>Fractional CFOs sit on a positioning spectrum that ranges from \"outsourced accounting with CFO in the title\" to \"strategic financial advisory that shapes major business decisions.\" The market price for the former is $2,000-$5,000 per month. The market price for the latter is $10,000-$25,000 per month. Both require roughly similar hours. The entire difference is in how you position the engagement, what deliverables you lead with, and whether you're talking to founders and boards (buyers at the premium end) or operations managers (buyers at the commodity end).</p><p>Premium positioning requires four things: a clear articulation of the strategic outcomes you deliver (not the tasks you perform), reference clients who can speak to those outcomes, the willingness to turn down engagements that would position you as a bookkeeper, and the patience to build toward the ideal client profile rather than accepting every opportunity that comes your way.</p><h2>Pricing Architecture</h2><p>Most fractional CFOs under-price for three reasons: hourly pricing psychology (they think in terms of hours rather than value), rate anchoring (they price based on what they earned as an employee plus a modest premium), and fear of rejection (they lower the price preemptively before the client even objects). The solution to all three is value-based pricing with a retainer structure.</p><p>A well-designed retainer engagement has three components: a defined scope of strategic services (what you will do), a defined set of outcomes you're accountable for (what success looks like), and a fixed monthly fee that reflects the value of those outcomes, not the hours required. When you price this way, a $15,000/month retainer is easy to justify if the client can see that you're protecting a $500K fundraising process, preventing a $200K tax mistake, or enabling a pricing decision worth $1M in annual revenue.</p><h2>Engagement Design for Leverage</h2><p>The way you structure engagements determines whether your practice is scalable or constrained. Single-client intensive engagements (10-15 days per month, one client) produce the highest income per engagement but limit total capacity to three to four clients. Portfolio engagements (3-5 days per month, eight to twelve clients) produce more predictable income and better risk diversification but require more systematized delivery. Most successful fractional CFOs start intensive and migrate toward portfolio as they build systems and a support team.</p><p>The key to a leveraged engagement model is documentation and delegation: every recurring deliverable should have a template, every recurring process should have a documented procedure, and every task below CFO-level should be delegated to a junior resource or handled by technology. The fractional CFO who does everything personally is constrained to four to five clients at most. The one who builds a practice infrastructure can serve eight to twelve while maintaining quality and working less total hours.</p><h2>Client Concentration and Risk</h2><p>A fractional CFO practice with two clients generating 70% of revenue has a concentration problem. If either client churns - a common event in growth-stage companies that get acquired, run out of money, or hire a full-time CFO - the practice faces a revenue crisis. Building to a minimum of six clients with no single client representing more than 25% of revenue is a reasonable target. This requires a continuous business development discipline, not just periodic bursts of outreach when the pipeline dries up.</p>",
