      "title": "The First 90 Days as Fractional CFO: Diagnosis, Priorities & Quick Wins",
      "module": "The CFO Mandate & Strategic Finance Leadership",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Why the First 90 Days Are Different From Every Other 90 Days</h2><p>The first 90 days of a fractional CFO engagement are unlike every subsequent period. You have maximum curiosity and minimum assumption bias - you're seeing the company fresh, and things that will later seem normal will now seem strange in ways that reveal important truths. You have implicit license to ask any question without seeming like you missed something. And you're still building the trust that will determine how much influence you have for the rest of the engagement. Wasting this window by doing routine financial work is one of the most expensive mistakes a fractional CFO can make.</p><p>The first 90 days should follow a structured sequence: diagnose first, prioritize second, deliver quick wins third, and only then build the operating model that will govern the long-term engagement. Companies that skip the diagnosis phase - because the CEO wants immediate action, or because the CFO is eager to prove their value - routinely spend months fixing the wrong problems and missing the real financial risks.</p><h2>The Diagnostic Phase: Days 1-30</h2><p>In the first 30 days, your primary deliverable is a complete financial diagnostic: a clear picture of the company's actual financial state versus its perceived financial state. These two things are almost never identical. Most founders have an intuitive sense of how the company is doing financially - a sense that is usually correct in direction but wrong in magnitude, missing important components, and systematically optimistic about the future.</p><p>The diagnostic covers seven dimensions: cash position and runway (the true number, not the founder's estimate), revenue quality (ARR vs. MRR, concentration, churn rate, contractual commitments), cost structure (fixed vs. variable, hidden costs, committed future obligations), unit economics (CAC, LTV, payback period, gross margin by segment), financial systems (accounting software, chart of accounts, close process, reporting quality), team and talent (who does what in finance, what are their capability gaps, what's documented vs. in someone's head), and financial risk (any legal, tax, or compliance exposures that need immediate attention).</p><h2>The Prioritization Framework: Days 31-60</h2><p>With the diagnostic complete, the second phase is prioritization. Not everything wrong with the financial operations needs to be fixed immediately, and some things that aren't broken yet will be unless they're addressed proactively. The right prioritization framework asks three questions: What creates existential risk if unaddressed? What creates the most value if addressed quickly? What builds the foundation for everything else?</p><p>Existential risks - a cash runway that's shorter than everyone realizes, a tax exposure that could trigger a penalty, a revenue recognition issue that could require financial restatement - go first regardless of difficulty. Value-creation opportunities - a pricing analysis that could increase revenue, a vendor renegotiation that could improve margin, a capital structure adjustment that could extend runway - go second. Foundation-building work - chart of accounts cleanup, reporting infrastructure, financial model rebuild - goes third, in the time remaining after the urgent work is done.</p><h2>Quick Wins: The Trust-Building Phase</h2><p>Quick wins in the first 90 days aren't primarily about financial impact - they're about trust. Every fractional CFO engagement starts with the client wondering whether they made the right choice. Quick wins are the evidence that settles that question in your favor. They need to be visible, connected to a problem the CEO already knew was a problem, and fast enough to deliver within the first 60 days.</p><p>The best quick wins are typically: a financial model that gives the CEO an accurate answer to a question they've been operating on instinct for (runway analysis, hiring impact, pricing scenarios), a vendor negotiation that produces a visible cost saving, or a financial control that closes an obvious gap. The specific win matters less than the fact that it's visible, fast, and addresses something the CEO was already worried about.</p><h2>Establishing the Long-Term Operating Model</h2><p>By day 90, the engagement should have moved from diagnostic mode to operating mode. The operating model is the agreement - explicit or implicit - about what the CFO will deliver, at what cadence, measured against what outcomes. Getting this agreement explicit and written before the engagement's first milestone is the difference between an engagement that renews automatically and one that requires a negotiation at every renewal. The best fractional CFO engagements are on autopilot at 90 days: the client knows what to expect, the CFO knows what's required, and the value is visible enough that the renewal question never arises.</p>",
