      "title": "ERP Selection & Implementation: What the CFO Must Control",
      "module": "Accounting Systems & Financial Architecture",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>The ERP Decision Is a Decade-Long Commitment</h2><p>Selecting an ERP system is among the most consequential financial infrastructure decisions a CFO makes. Unlike most technology choices, which can be reversed with moderate effort, an ERP implementation creates deep organizational dependencies: data architectures, workflows, integrations, user behaviors, and reporting structures that become embedded over years. The average mid-market company keeps its ERP for 8-12 years. The system selected this year will be running financial operations for a company that may be five times larger, in multiple geographies, with product lines that don't yet exist. The CFO who selects for current needs rather than future architecture is signing up for a painful migration at the worst possible time - typically during a period of rapid growth when the business can least afford operational disruption.</p><p>For fractional CFOs, ERP decisions arise in three contexts: companies that have outgrown QuickBooks and need to migrate to a cloud ERP, companies that are on a system that no longer fits their business model, and post-M&A integration situations where two systems need to become one. Each context has different urgency and different decision criteria, but the fundamental evaluation methodology is the same.</p><h2>Building the Requirements Before Evaluating Vendors</h2><p>The most common ERP selection failure is vendor-first evaluation: scheduling demos before documenting what the business actually needs the system to do. Vendors are excellent at showing their strongest features; they are less excellent at transparently disclosing where their system requires workarounds or customization for your specific use case. When you haven't defined your requirements before the demo, you're evaluating what the vendor chose to show you - not what you need.</p><p>Building requirements starts with process mapping: what are the 20-30 most important financial and operational processes the system must support? For each, what does the current state look like, what does the desired future state look like, and what are the volume and complexity parameters? Requirements should be prioritized into must-have, should-have, and nice-to-have - a framework that gives evaluators the ability to make trade-offs without the emotional pressure of treating all requirements as equally critical.</p><h2>The CFO-Controlled Evaluation Process</h2><p>The CFO must personally lead the ERP evaluation - not delegate it to IT. The reason is fundamental: the CFO is the primary user of the system's financial outputs, and only the CFO can evaluate whether the system will produce the management information the business needs at the quality and speed required. IT can evaluate technical architecture, security, and integration capability. They cannot evaluate whether the consolidated P&L view, the segment reporting structure, or the intercompany reconciliation workflow will meet the CFO's standards. Delegating this decision to IT produces technically sound implementations that fail at the management reporting level.</p><p>The evaluation process should include: a scripted demo that tests specific use cases from your requirements list (not the vendor's standard demo flow), reference calls with three to five customers of similar size and complexity who have been live on the system for at least 18 months, a detailed total cost of ownership analysis over five years, and a contract negotiation that includes specific go-live milestones, remediation rights if the system doesn't perform as represented, and data portability provisions for future migration.</p><h2>Implementation Governance</h2><p>ERP implementations fail most often not during selection but during implementation. The CFO's role during implementation is governance, not project management: attending weekly steering committee meetings, making go/no-go decisions at each phase milestone, and having the authority to extend the timeline if quality is not sufficient for go-live. The single biggest mistake CFOs make during implementation is deferring to the vendor's timeline pressure. Vendors have incentives to complete the project and move to the next engagement; the CFO's incentive is to go live on a system that actually works. These incentives are not aligned.</p><p>Post-go-live, budget explicitly for a hyper-care period: three to six months of intensive support, enhanced reconciliation processes, and parallel-run reporting to validate that the new system is producing correct outputs. Companies that skip this period - going live and immediately decommissioning the old system - routinely discover discrepancies in the second or third month that require expensive remediation under operational pressure.</p>",
