      "content": "<h2>Audit Readiness Is Year-Round, Not Year-End</h2><p>Most companies treat audit preparation as an annual sprint: the auditors announce their fieldwork dates, and the finance team spends the preceding three weeks scrambling to pull documentation, reconcile accounts, and answer questions they should have been able to answer all year. This approach is expensive (more audit hours = higher fees), stressful (the team is stretched at year-end when they're already busy with close and planning), and risky (scrambled preparation leads to errors and omissions that extend the audit). Audit-ready companies - those that can hand the auditors a complete, organized package on day one of fieldwork - achieve faster audits, lower fees, and cleaner opinions.</p><p>Building audit readiness as a year-round practice requires three things: accounts maintained in reconciled, documented condition throughout the year (not just at year-end); a control environment that produces evidence of its operation continuously; and a prepared-by-client (PBC) list that is developed before the audit, not responded to reactively when the auditors send their requests.</p><h2>The COSO Framework and Why CFOs Must Know It</h2><p>The Committee of Sponsoring Organizations (COSO) Internal Control framework is the primary conceptual model for internal control design and evaluation. Auditors evaluate your controls against it; sophisticated investors ask about it; and SOX compliance (for public companies) is organized around it. The framework has five components: control environment (the tone at the top and the organizational commitment to integrity and competence), risk assessment (the process by which the company identifies and analyzes risks to its financial reporting objectives), control activities (the specific policies and procedures designed to address those risks), information and communication (the systems that capture and transmit relevant information), and monitoring (the ongoing processes that evaluate whether controls are operating effectively).</p><p>For a growth-stage company, the most important COSO application is control activities: what specific controls prevent or detect material misstatement in the financial statements? The CFO must be able to describe the key controls for each significant financial statement line item and provide evidence that those controls operated throughout the year.</p><h2>Designing Key Controls</h2><p>Effective internal control design starts with the risk: what could go wrong in this financial statement area? For each significant risk, there should be at least one key control - a procedure that, if it operates effectively, prevents or detects the risk. Controls should be both preventive (preventing errors from occurring) and detective (catching errors that do occur). Over-relying on detective controls means errors reach the financial statements before being caught; over-relying on preventive controls means the control environment is brittle if any single control fails.</p><p>For a CFO designing controls for a $20M revenue company, the minimum key controls typically include: bank reconciliation (monthly, with CFO review of all reconciling items over a threshold), expense report approval (manager approval required, CFO approval above a threshold), vendor payment approval (dual approval for payments above a threshold), revenue recognition review (monthly review of contract modifications and judgmental estimates), and financial statement review (CFO review of draft statements before distribution). Each control should have documented evidence of its operation: approval signatures, reconciliation sign-offs, review meeting notes.</p><h2>Separation of Duties in Small Finance Teams</h2><p>The standard control design principle - the person who authorizes a transaction should not be the same person who records and reconciles it - is straightforward in large organizations with deep accounting teams. In small companies with two or three finance staff, full separation is often impossible. The CFO's role is to design compensating controls: enhanced supervisory review, surprise reconciliations, and increased access monitoring that substitute for the segregation that isn't achievable given team size. When compensating controls are designed with this explicit intention, auditors can accept them. When they're not documented and explained, auditors flag them as control deficiencies.</p><h2>Preparing for First-Time Audits</h2><p>Companies receiving their first institutional investment typically face their first financial statement audit within the year following closing. The CFO should use the pre-audit period to address three things: ensure the financial statements are prepared in accordance with GAAP (not just income tax basis), build out the control environment documentation, and develop relationships with audit firms that have relevant expertise. The auditor selection decision - often made under time pressure - has multi-year implications. Choosing a firm that is too large creates cost and bandwidth mismatches; too small creates risk of the firm being outgrown. The right selection is a firm with SaaS or growth-company expertise at a size appropriate for your company's complexity.</p>",
