      "content": "<h2>Why 13 Weeks Is the Right Horizon</h2><p>Cash flow forecasting exists on a spectrum from single-day treasury management to five-year strategic projections. The 13-week model occupies a unique and critical position on that spectrum: long enough to give meaningful warning of a cash problem, short enough that the inputs are reliably estimable. Beyond 13 weeks, forecasting uncertainty compounds rapidly for most operating businesses - the further out you go, the more assumptions stack up and the less the forecast resembles actual cash flows. Within 13 weeks, the major cash flows are largely visible: payroll dates are known, major vendor payments are scheduled, and the receivables pipeline has reasonable visibility.</p><p>For CFOs managing cash-constrained businesses - which describes most growth-stage companies at some point in their development - the 13-week model is not an optional analytical exercise. It is the primary risk management tool. A cash problem visible 13 weeks in advance can be solved through a combination of accelerated collections, deferred discretionary spending, and proactive lender communication. The same problem with two weeks of visibility requires emergency measures that are both expensive and damaging to vendor and employee relationships.</p><h2>Model Architecture</h2><p>The 13-week cash flow model forecasts cash receipts and disbursements for each of the next 13 calendar weeks, producing an ending cash balance for each week. The model operates differently from an accrual P&L: it captures cash when it moves, not when it's earned or incurred. A $500K invoice sent on March 1 appears in the P&L in March but appears in the 13-week model in the week the customer actually pays - which might be April or May depending on payment terms and customer behavior.</p><p>The model has three sections. Receipts: expected cash inflows from customer payments, organized by customer or customer segment, with payment timing based on contractual terms and historical payment behavior. Disbursements: expected cash outflows organized into categories - payroll (which is fixed and highly predictable), accounts payable (scheduled based on invoice due dates and payment policies), and discretionary spending (which can be managed if needed). Net cash flow: the weekly difference between receipts and disbursements, which cumulates to the ending cash balance.</p><h2>Building the Receipts Section</h2><p>The receipts section is where most 13-week models fail. There are two common errors: using invoiced amounts instead of expected payment timing, and assuming all customers pay on time. The model should use collections behavior, not billing behavior. If your average customer pays 45 days after invoice, a March 1 invoice doesn't become cash until mid-April. Organizing the receipts section by payment lag rather than billing date produces a dramatically more accurate forecast.</p><p>For each major customer, maintain a collection history: average days from invoice to payment, range of actual payment timing, and any signals about upcoming payment delays (customer financial stress, disputed invoices, contract renegotiations). This history should feed the forecast directly. For smaller customers, use statistical averages from historical collections data. The forecast should be updated weekly with actuals - a discipline that continuously improves forecast accuracy and keeps the CFO aware of developing collection problems.</p><h2>Building the Disbursements Section</h2><p>Disbursements have different predictability levels. Payroll is essentially certain: the dates are known, the amounts are approximately known (variable components add modest uncertainty), and there is no practical ability to defer it without serious consequences. Payroll should always be modeled as a hard commitment. Accounts payable has more flexibility: vendor terms, early payment discounts, and payment scheduling decisions affect timing. The model should separate committed AP (invoices due with hard payment terms) from manageable AP (payments that can be deferred in a cash constraint scenario). Discretionary spending - travel, events, marketing - should be identified as a line item with a footnote indicating the portion that could be reduced or deferred in a tightening scenario.</p><h2>The Scenario Overlay</h2><p>The most valuable 13-week model has two layers: the base case (most likely outcomes) and a stress scenario (a plausible downside that tests resilience). The stress scenario typically assumes 10-20% of receivables being collected 15-30 days late and two or three major disbursements hitting simultaneously. If the stress scenario shows the company going cash-negative within 13 weeks, the CFO needs to act immediately - not wait to see whether the stress scenario materializes. The 13-week model is a trigger system as much as it is a forecasting tool.</p>",
