      "content": "<h2>Collections as a Revenue Function</h2><p>Accounts receivable management is frequently classified as a back-office administrative function and staffed accordingly. This classification creates an expensive blind spot: uncollected receivables are the most common cash flow problem in growing businesses, and an underpowered collections function allows the problem to compound silently until it becomes a crisis. The CFO who treats collections as a revenue function - with clear metrics, dedicated focus, and escalation protocols - eliminates one of the most preventable sources of cash shortfall.</p><p>The distinction between poor collections and poor credit judgment is important. Poor collections means receivables that should be collected aren't being chased effectively. Poor credit judgment means receivables that were created from customers unlikely to pay. Both create DSO problems, but they require different solutions. Poor collections is a process problem; poor credit judgment is an underwriting problem. A rigorous CFO diagnoses which they have before prescribing the fix.</p><h2>The Collections Process Architecture</h2><p>An effective B2B collections process has five stages organized around the invoice lifecycle. Stage one is proactive communication: sending the invoice with all required information (purchase order number, approved contact, correct billing entity) and confirming receipt, ideally within 24 hours of invoice. Most payment delays start with an invoice that arrives with incorrect or missing information that the customer uses as justification to pause the payment process.</p><p>Stage two is early follow-up: a non-threatening check-in at 50-60% of the due date. \"Hi [name], just confirming invoice #12345 is in your payment queue for [due date].\" This converts potential problems - lost invoices, missing PO numbers, approval bottlenecks - into solvable issues before the due date. Stage three is due-date follow-up: direct contact on or one day before the due date if payment hasn't arrived. Stage four is escalation for overdue accounts: a structured cadence of increasingly senior contacts - starting with the AP manager and escalating to the CFO-to-CFO call if needed. Stage five is dispute resolution or write-off: formal handling of disputed invoices and the CFO's decision about when uncollectable amounts warrant a write-off.</p><h2>Credit Policy and Underwriting</h2><p>Prevention is the best collections strategy. A credit policy that prevents the company from extending significant credit to customers unlikely to pay eliminates collection problems at their source. A functional credit policy addresses three things: credit limits by customer segment (how much exposure is acceptable given the customer's size and creditworthiness?), payment terms by customer type (do all customers get the same terms, or do terms reflect risk?), and credit review triggers (when does an account require a fresh credit assessment - after a certain volume of business, after a late payment, or on a scheduled basis?).</p><p>For B2B SaaS businesses, credit policy is simpler because subscription contracts typically require payment before service delivery (prepaid annual or monthly billing). For businesses that invoice after service delivery, credit policy is essential. Requesting a Dun & Bradstreet report or Experian business credit report before extending material credit is standard practice and costs far less than the average bad debt write-off.</p><h2>AR Aging Analysis</h2><p>The AR aging report - a listing of all outstanding invoices organized by how long they've been outstanding - is the primary management tool for collections. The CFO should review the aging weekly, with explicit attention to the 60+ and 90+ day buckets. Most healthy businesses have less than 5% of AR in the 90+ bucket; a company with 20% or more of its AR over 90 days has a collections dysfunction that needs immediate diagnosis and intervention. The aging should be reviewed with the collections team, not just received as a report - the qualitative context (this customer always pays late, this invoice is disputed, this account is at risk) is essential for making good collection decisions.</p>",
