      "content": "<h2>Why SaaS Metrics Are Different From Traditional Financial Metrics</h2><p>SaaS businesses are fundamentally different from traditional businesses in a way that makes standard financial metrics misleading. In a traditional business, revenue in a period represents value created in that period. In a SaaS business, revenue in a period represents partial recognition of value created when the contract was signed - months or years earlier. A SaaS company can be growing its contracted value (ARR) rapidly while its recognized revenue grows slowly because of deferred recognition. Conversely, it can be losing customers and shrinking its contracted base while recognized revenue stays flat for a quarter as existing contracts run out. Standard income statement analysis misses these dynamics entirely. SaaS metrics are designed to see what GAAP financials hide.</p><h2>The Core SaaS Metric Stack</h2><p>Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR) is the north star: the annualized value of all active subscription contracts. It tells you what the revenue run rate is today, regardless of when those contracts were signed or how they're being recognized. Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR) is ARR divided by 12 - useful for monthly reporting and for tracking in-period movements. The MRR bridge is the most important ARR analysis tool: it decomposes the change in MRR into new logos (new customers), expansion (existing customers buying more), contraction (existing customers buying less), and churn (customers who canceled). This four-way decomposition tells the complete story of revenue health.</p><p>Net Revenue Retention (NRR) measures how the cohort of customers from a prior period is performing today, including expansion and churn effects. An NRR above 100% means the existing customer base is growing on its own - even with zero new customer acquisition, revenue would increase. NRR is the single metric that best predicts the long-term financial health of a SaaS business; the best SaaS companies consistently achieve NRR of 110-140%. Gross Revenue Retention (GRR) measures the same cohort but excludes expansion - it shows how much of last year's revenue base survived without accounting for upsell. GRR below 80% is a serious warning signal.</p><h2>Unit Economics: CAC, LTV, and Payback</h2><p>Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) is the fully loaded cost of acquiring one new customer: all sales and marketing expense in a period divided by the number of new customers acquired. Lifetime Value (LTV) is the expected cumulative gross profit generated by a customer over their relationship with the company. The LTV:CAC ratio measures the return on customer acquisition investment - a ratio above 3:1 indicates healthy unit economics; below 2:1 suggests the acquisition model needs work. CAC payback period - the months required to recover the acquisition cost through gross profit - should be under 12-18 months for most SaaS businesses; above 24 months indicates capital inefficiency that will constrain growth.</p><h2>Modeling SaaS Growth</h2><p>A well-built SaaS financial model forecasts from unit economics up: starting with customer acquisition assumptions (how many new customers will be acquired each month, at what CAC), adding expansion and churn assumptions (what percentage of existing customers will expand, at what ACV, and what percentage will churn), and deriving ARR, recognized revenue, and cash flow from these inputs. This bottom-up approach allows the model to test the relationship between growth investment and ARR growth - a critical input for fundraising conversations where investors want to understand the capital efficiency of growth.</p><h2>Cohort Analysis</h2><p>Cohort analysis - tracking groups of customers acquired in the same period over time - is the most powerful analytical tool in SaaS. A retention curve that shows how many customers from each monthly cohort are still active at 6, 12, 24, and 36 months reveals the true health of the customer relationship far better than aggregate churn statistics. A company with 95% monthly retention retains about 54% of its customers over 12 months - a healthy outcome. A company with 92% monthly retention retains only 37% at 12 months - a very different business. The difference of 3 percentage points in monthly retention has a dramatic cumulative effect that cohort analysis makes visible.</p>",
