      "title": "Debt Financing: Lines of Credit, Term Loans & Structured Finance",
      "module": "Capital Structure & Fundraising",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>When Debt Is the Right Capital Source</h2><p>The CFO's first question when a company needs capital isn't \"how do we raise equity?\" - it's \"is debt available, and is it cheaper than equity for this specific use case?\" In many situations, debt is both available and dramatically cheaper than equity. The company that funds a $1M expansion of proven revenue-generating operations with a term loan at 8% preserves equity that is worth multiples of that in a future fundraising event. The company that reflexively raises equity for every capital need is systematically over-diluting its founders, employees, and early investors.</p><p>Debt is appropriate when the use of capital has a predictable, time-bounded return that is sufficient to service the obligation. Equipment acquisition, receivables financing, real estate, and working capital needs tied to contracted revenue are all candidates for debt rather than equity. Equity is appropriate for investments with highly uncertain outcomes - new product development, market expansion into unknown territory, early-stage hiring - where the return profile doesn't support the certainty of debt service.</p><h2>Revolving Lines of Credit</h2><p>A revolving line of credit is the most flexible form of debt: the company can draw up to its maximum commitment, repay, and draw again as needed. Interest accrues only on outstanding balances. Revolvers are ideal for managing working capital fluctuations - drawing during periods of high receivables (when cash lags revenue) and repaying when collections accelerate. Most revolvers have annual renewal provisions, which means the bank can choose not to renew even if the company is in compliance; this creates an implicit pressure to maintain a strong banking relationship throughout the facility term, not just at origination.</p><p>The key terms to negotiate in a revolver: commitment size (larger is better but banks charge a commitment fee on undrawn balances), advance rate and eligible receivable definitions (if it's an asset-based revolver, what percentage of receivables count as eligible collateral?), pricing (SOFR plus a spread), and covenant package. A revolver with a borrowing base certificate requirement - monthly submission of receivables and payables data to calculate available credit - creates administrative work but typically produces better credit terms since the lender has current visibility into the collateral base.</p><h2>Term Loans</h2><p>Term loans provide a fixed amount with a defined repayment schedule - typically monthly or quarterly principal payments over 3-7 years. They're appropriate for specific capital investments: equipment purchases, technology implementations, acquisitions, and expansion projects where the expected cash returns over a defined period can service the scheduled payments. Unlike revolvers, term loans fully amortize: the balance decreases with each payment and cannot be re-drawn. For this reason, the cost of a term loan includes the opportunity cost of the equity that would have been preserved by using debt - a calculation that favors term loans when the expected return on the investment funded materially exceeds the interest rate.</p><p>Term loan covenants are typically more stringent than revolver covenants because the lender has a longer-dated, less liquid exposure. Financial covenants - minimum EBITDA, maximum leverage, minimum fixed charge coverage - should be negotiated with realistic headroom: not just enough to comply under the base case, but enough to comply under a moderate downside. A 10% miss against plan triggering a covenant violation is a management distraction the company doesn't need when it's already navigating underperformance.</p><h2>Structured Finance and Alternative Debt</h2><p>Beyond traditional bank debt, the structured finance market offers several instruments relevant to CFOs of growth-stage companies. Revenue-based financing provides capital repaid as a percentage of monthly revenue rather than fixed principal - attractive when cash flows are lumpy or uncertain. Invoice factoring advances cash against outstanding receivables, typically at 70-85% of face value, with the factoring company collecting from the customer directly. Asset-based lending against specific assets - intellectual property, customer contracts, equipment - provides access to capital for companies that don't qualify for traditional bank debt on cash flow coverage alone.</p><p>The CFO evaluating alternative debt structures must always calculate the all-in effective annual cost, which is often buried in fee structures, discount rates, and origination charges. A revenue-based facility that \"costs 6%\" of funded capital as a flat fee may actually represent a 15-25% effective annual rate depending on the repayment timeline. Comparing apples to apples requires converting every debt instrument to its effective annual cost and comparing it to both the cost of equity and the expected return on the funded investment.</p>",
