      "content": "<h2>Why Cybersecurity Is a CFO Issue</h2><p>Cybersecurity risk has migrated from the IT department's concern to the CFO's balance sheet in the last decade. The financial consequences of a material cyber incident - customer data breach, ransomware attack, business email compromise, intellectual property theft - are now routinely measured in millions of dollars: incident response costs, regulatory fines, customer notification costs, lost revenue during outages, and litigation. For CFOs who have spent their careers on financial risk, cyber represents a relatively new category of financial risk that requires the same analytical treatment as any other material exposure.</p><p>Most CFOs are uncomfortable with cybersecurity risk because they lack the technical vocabulary to engage with it confidently. The solution is not to acquire deep technical expertise - that's the CISO's job - but to develop the ability to translate technical risk into financial risk, which is the CFO's domain. The FAIR (Factor Analysis of Information Risk) model provides a structured framework for quantifying cyber risk in financial terms: expected frequency of loss events multiplied by probable loss magnitude produces a dollar-denominated risk estimate that CFOs and boards can engage with.</p><h2>Quantifying Cyber Risk</h2><p>The FAIR model decomposes cyber risk into two components: Loss Event Frequency (how often might this type of attack succeed against this company in a given year?) and Loss Magnitude (if an attack succeeds, what is the expected financial impact?). Loss magnitude has six sub-components: response costs (incident response, forensics, notification), legal costs (regulatory fines, litigation, settlements), productivity loss (revenue during outage, employee remediation time), competitive advantage loss (IP theft, lost deals), reputation damage (customer churn, brand impairment), and replacement costs (hardware, software, infrastructure). Assembling these components with order-of-magnitude estimates - even rough ones - produces a risk quantification that is more useful for decision-making than a qualitative rating of \"high/medium/low.\"</p><p>The CFO should work with the CISO to develop financial estimates for the company's top three to five cyber scenarios: a data breach exposing customer personal information, a ransomware attack that encrypts critical systems, a business email compromise resulting in fraudulent wire transfers, and any industry-specific scenarios relevant to the business. Each scenario should have a low, expected, and high loss estimate. These estimates drive two important decisions: how much to invest in prevention controls (justified up to the expected value of the risk prevented), and how much cyber insurance to carry (sized to cover the expected loss plus a prudent buffer).</p><h2>Communicating Cyber Risk to the Board</h2><p>The board's oversight responsibility for cybersecurity has grown significantly in response to SEC guidance and shareholder expectations. The CFO and CISO should jointly present cybersecurity to the board at least quarterly, covering: the current threat environment (what attacks are targeting companies like ours?), the company's control posture (what are we doing to prevent and detect attacks?), the financial exposure quantification (what is the expected loss range for our top scenarios?), the cyber insurance program (is coverage adequate for the quantified exposure?), and any recent incidents or near-misses that provide insight into actual threat activity.</p><p>The CFO-specific contributions to this presentation are the financial quantification and the insurance analysis. CISO presentations that don't include financial figures are incomplete board communications - board members who receive technical descriptions of threats without financial context cannot make informed governance decisions about cybersecurity investment levels. The CFO who can translate the CISO's technical assessment into a financial risk range has filled a critical gap in board-level cyber governance.</p><h2>Cyber Insurance Design</h2><p>Cyber insurance has become a standard element of corporate risk management but requires careful policy review to understand what is and isn't covered. Standard first-party coverages include breach response costs, ransomware payments, business interruption losses, and cyber extortion. Third-party coverages include regulatory defense costs, fines and penalties (where insurable under applicable law), and liability to customers for data breach damages. Key exclusions to understand: war exclusions (cyber attacks attributed to nation-states may be excluded - a significant gap given state-sponsored attack frequency), infrastructure exclusions (damage to shared critical infrastructure), and social engineering sublimits (business email compromise coverage is often capped well below the main policy limit). The CFO should review the policy with a specialized cyber insurance broker annually, as coverage terms in this market evolve rapidly in response to escalating claims experience.</p>",
