      "title": "100-Day Integration Finance Playbook",
      "module": "M&A, Due Diligence & Integration Finance",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>The Integration Period Determines Whether the Deal Creates Value</h2><p>The research on M&A value creation consistently shows that most acquisitions fail to create the value that justified their purchase price. The most common reason is not a bad acquisition thesis - it's poor integration execution. The 100 days following a close are when the strategic rationale must be translated into operational reality. Synergies don't realize themselves; they require deliberate plans, dedicated resources, and clear accountability. The CFO who doesn't own a rigorous integration finance workstream is watching value evaporate at a pace that the financial statements won't reveal for another 12-18 months.</p><p>The CFO's integration finance responsibilities begin before close and extend through the first year post-close. Pre-close: financial systems mapping, accounting policy alignment, and Day 1 readiness. Days 1-30: financial reporting consolidation, immediate synergy captures that don't require integration complexity, and the establishment of the integration management office (IMO) financial tracking. Days 31-60: deeper financial systems integration, working capital management under combined operations, and the first update to synergy targets with actual integration experience informing estimates. Days 61-100: initial synergy scorecard versus plan, revised full-year forecast incorporating acquisition results, and the transition from integration mode to steady-state operations.</p><h2>Financial Reporting Consolidation</h2><p>On Day 1 post-close, the acquired entity becomes a subsidiary whose financial results must be consolidated into the parent's financial statements. The consolidation mechanics require: chart of accounts alignment (the acquired entity's accounts must be mapped to the parent's structure), intercompany elimination (transactions between the parent and subsidiary must be eliminated in consolidation), purchase accounting (the acquisition fair values all acquired assets and liabilities, creating new depreciation and amortization schedules), and goodwill recognition (the excess of purchase price over fair value of net assets is recorded as goodwill). The CFO should ensure these mechanics are designed and tested before the close date - not discovered in the first week of consolidation under time pressure.</p><h2>Synergy Tracking</h2><p>Synergy realization must be tracked with the same rigor as revenue and cost targets. The synergy tracker - a monthly report showing each committed synergy, its target realization timeline, current status, actual-versus-target to date, and the estimated full-year impact - is the primary accountability mechanism for integration value creation. Every synergy must have a named owner who is accountable for delivering it. The CFO should present the synergy tracker to the board quarterly - not as a success narrative, but as a genuine accountability mechanism that acknowledges shortfalls early enough for course correction.</p><h2>Integration Cost Tracking</h2><p>Acquisition integration always costs more than the pre-close estimate, and CFOs who don't track integration costs separately from normal operating expenses miss this pattern until it's too late to manage it. Integration costs - severance, system migration, facility consolidation, retention bonuses, legal and advisory fees - should be coded to a dedicated integration cost center that provides full visibility into the total investment being made to capture the planned synergies. When integration costs are running ahead of synergy realization, the ratio of cost to benefit becomes visible and manageable. When they're commingled with operating expenses, the problem is invisible until the total acquisition economics are reviewed retrospectively.</p>",
