      "content": "<h2>Unit Economics Define Scalability</h2><p>Unit economics - the revenue and cost associated with a single unit of business, whether that's a customer, a transaction, or a product sold - are the most predictive financial metrics available for assessing whether a business model is fundamentally healthy. A business with strong unit economics becomes more profitable as it scales; a business with weak unit economics loses money faster as it scales, no matter how impressive the revenue growth looks from the outside. The CFO who can articulate unit economics clearly is the CFO who can tell the board whether the growth they're investing in will create or destroy value at scale.</p><p>For SaaS and subscription businesses, the unit economics framework centers on four metrics: CAC (customer acquisition cost - the fully loaded cost to acquire one new customer), LTV (lifetime value - the expected cumulative gross profit from one customer over their relationship with the company), LTV:CAC ratio (the return multiple on the acquisition investment), and CAC payback period (the months required to recover the acquisition cost in gross profit). For transaction businesses (e-commerce, marketplace, financial services), the relevant unit is the transaction itself: contribution margin per transaction, transaction frequency, and customer lifetime value through repeat purchase rates.</p><h2>The Contribution Margin Framework</h2><p>Contribution margin - revenue minus variable costs - is the bridge between unit economics and P&L economics. A product with a 70% contribution margin means that for every dollar of incremental revenue, 70 cents flows to cover fixed costs and ultimately profit. Understanding contribution margin at the unit level (per customer, per transaction, per SKU) is essential for growth decisions: if marketing investment in segment A produces customers with 60% contribution margins while segment B produces customers with 40% margins, the company should weight its acquisition investment toward segment A - even if the CAC is identical - because the LTV difference creates a superior return.</p><p>Contribution margin analysis must be fully loaded to be useful. A common error is excluding the cost of customer success, support, and account management from the contribution margin calculation on the grounds that these are \"period costs.\" For SaaS businesses where these costs are essential to retaining and growing the customer relationship, they are unit costs - they vary with the customer base, even if they don't vary with each individual transaction. Excluding them produces overstated contribution margins that lead to overstated LTV calculations and overinvestment in customer acquisition.</p><h2>The CAC Decomposition</h2><p>CAC is often treated as a single number, but decomposing it reveals the specific levers available for improvement. CAC = (Sales + Marketing spend) / New customers acquired. But this aggregate obscures channel-level differences: a company that acquires 60% of customers through outbound sales at $8,000 CAC and 40% through inbound content marketing at $1,200 CAC has a blended CAC of $5,280 - a number that understates the cost of the outbound channel and overstates the cost of the inbound channel. Channel-level CAC analysis enables informed decisions about channel mix: shifting investment toward lower-CAC channels (if they can absorb more volume) directly improves blended CAC and extends the LTV:CAC ratio without requiring any change in the underlying product or pricing.</p><h2>Cohort Economics at Scale</h2><p>The true test of unit economics at scale is whether early cohorts' performance is predictive of later cohorts'. A company that saw excellent unit economics in its first 100 customers - high LTV, low churn, strong expansion - but sees deteriorating metrics in more recent cohorts may be exhausting its best-fit customer segment. Cohort comparison analysis - comparing retention curves, expansion rates, and payback periods across cohort vintages - reveals whether the business is maintaining its economic model at scale or whether customer quality is degrading as acquisition reaches into less ideal segments. This is the analysis the CFO should produce when the board asks \"are the unit economics sustainable at $100M ARR?\"</p>",
