      "title": "Make vs. Buy vs. Partner: Financial Decision Architecture",
      "module": "Strategic Finance & Value Creation",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Every Capability Decision Is a Financial Decision</h2><p>Make vs. buy vs. partner decisions are among the most recurring strategic choices in a growing business, and they are systematically underdisciplined. Companies default to building (make) when they believe the capability is core, outsourcing (buy) when they're under budget pressure, and partnering when they're not sure what to do. None of these defaults is analytically grounded - and the CFO who builds a rigorous financial framework for these decisions creates a competitive advantage in resource allocation that compounds over years of better choices.</p><p>The financial analysis of make vs. buy vs. partner requires comparing total cost of ownership across all three options, including costs that are frequently ignored in informal analyses. The make option typically involves hiring, training, and retaining specialized talent, plus the opportunity cost of management attention diverted from core activities. The buy option requires vendor selection, contract negotiation, ongoing management, and the switching costs if the relationship needs to change. The partner option involves negotiating the partnership economics, managing joint go-to-market activities, and giving up some portion of the revenue or margin to the partner. All three have a total economic cost that extends well beyond the line item that appears on the budget request.</p><h2>The Core vs. Context Framework</h2><p>Geoffrey Moore's core vs. context framework provides a useful first filter for make vs. buy vs. partner decisions. Core activities are those where differentiation drives competitive advantage - activities the company does better than competitors in ways that customers notice and pay for. Context activities are everything else: necessary but not differentiating. The financial principle follows directly: invest in making core activities better (make), outsource context activities efficiently (buy), and partner for capabilities that are core to the partner but context to you (partner).</p><p>For a CFO applying this framework, the question is always: \"Is this capability a source of our competitive advantage, or is it infrastructure that enables our competitive advantage?\" A SaaS company's software development is core; its payroll processing is context. A consulting firm's methodology is core; its CRM system is context. Building and maintaining context capabilities with core-level investment is a common and expensive resource misallocation that the make vs. buy framework makes visible.</p><h2>Total Cost of Ownership Analysis</h2><p>TCO analysis for a make vs. buy decision should cover five to seven years and include all relevant costs. For the make option: recruiting and hiring cost (typically 15-20% of first-year compensation for professional roles), ramp time before productivity (the months during which the hire is below full productivity), compensation and benefits (fully loaded, including employer taxes and equity), management overhead (the fraction of manager time required to supervise and develop the hire), and productivity risk (what happens if the hire underperforms or leaves?). For the buy option: implementation cost, annual subscription or service fees, internal management time, switching costs, and vendor dependency risk. The comparison often reveals that make is more expensive than expected (due to hidden costs), buy is less expensive than expected (especially for SaaS tools that have replaced expensive custom development), and partner options are rarely modeled with full rigor because the partner relationship feels intangible.</p><h2>Partnership Economics</h2><p>Partnerships deserve the same financial rigor as build and buy decisions. The CFO should model the economics of any significant partnership: what revenue does the partner generate or enable, what does the company contribute to the partnership (product, marketing, brand), and what is the partner's share of the economics? Partnerships that look attractive in narrative form often reveal poor economics when modeled: a reseller relationship with 30% partner margins on $1M of potential revenue generates $700K of net revenue - only attractive if the cost of direct sales to those customers would have exceeded the $300K partner margin. Making that comparison explicit is the CFO's contribution to partnership strategy.</p>",
