      "title": "Pricing Strategy for CFOs: The Revenue-Margin Optimization Tradeoff",
      "module": "Strategic Finance & Value Creation",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Pricing Is the Highest-Leverage Revenue Lever</h2><p>Of all the variables available to improve a company's financial performance, pricing is theoretically the most powerful and practically the most underused. A 1% improvement in pricing typically produces a 10-15% improvement in operating profit, because the entire pricing improvement flows to the bottom line with no additional cost. By contrast, a 1% improvement in volume requires additional cost of sales, cost of goods, and often additional fixed cost investment. Yet most companies spend enormous effort on volume growth and almost no systematic effort on pricing optimization. The CFO who brings analytical rigor to pricing decisions is unlocking one of the highest-return levers in the business.</p><p>The CFO's role in pricing is analytical and advisory - the revenue and product teams own the customer relationships that determine pricing sensitivity, but the CFO provides the financial model that quantifies the revenue-margin trade-off of any pricing decision. This requires understanding price elasticity (how does demand change when price changes?), competitive dynamics (what alternatives do customers have?), and the mechanics of the pricing change (which customers are affected, over what timeline, and at what risk of churn?).</p><h2>Price Elasticity Analysis</h2><p>Price elasticity measures how sensitive customer demand is to price changes. An elasticity of -1.5 means a 10% price increase reduces demand by 15% - in that range, raising prices reduces revenue. An elasticity of -0.5 means a 10% price increase reduces demand by 5% - raising prices increases revenue. For most B2B SaaS products, particularly those with high switching costs and strong NRR, elasticity tends to be relatively low (between 0 and -1), meaning modest price increases are revenue-positive. For commodity products with many substitutes, elasticity is typically higher (more negative), making price increases more dangerous.</p><p>Empirical elasticity testing - actually changing prices in controlled experiments or across customer segments and measuring the impact - is more reliable than survey-based willingness-to-pay research for B2B products. Customers tell surveys what they think they should be willing to pay; they show you what they're actually willing to pay in their purchase and renewal decisions. The CFO should advocate for controlled pricing experiments that generate real data on elasticity before the company makes large-scale pricing changes based on assumptions.</p><h2>Pricing Architecture</h2><p>Pricing architecture - how pricing tiers, bundles, and packages are designed - often has more impact on revenue than the specific price points within that architecture. Good pricing architecture aligns customer value capture with company revenue capture: customers who derive more value pay more. Common architectural choices include: good/better/best tiering (offering three packages at escalating price points), usage-based pricing (pricing that scales with consumption), per-seat licensing (pricing that scales with organizational adoption), and outcome-based pricing (pricing tied to the specific value the customer realizes). Each architecture has different implications for revenue predictability, expansion revenue potential, and customer success investment requirements.</p><h2>CFO Price Increase Analysis</h2><p>When the company decides to raise prices on its existing customer base - a more complex decision than setting prices for new customers - the CFO must model three scenarios: the volume impact (how many customers will churn due to the price increase?), the timing impact (will customers accelerate renewals before the increase takes effect, creating a short-term revenue boost followed by a drought?), and the net revenue impact (what is the expected annual recurring revenue impact net of churn, accounting for the higher price on retained customers?). A price increase that raises ARPU by 15% while increasing churn by 5% may or may not be revenue-positive depending on the revenue mix - the CFO's model makes this explicit and informs the decision with quantified expectations rather than optimistic assumptions.</p>",
